+# QA report: newsletter signup says "You're subscribed" when it isn't
+
+## Summary
+
+The signup page shows **"You're subscribed. Check your inbox for the first issue."** for any server response, including the server's `422` rejection. The address is never stored, so no email goes out, but the user is told it worked.
+
+## Root cause
+
+`public/app.js:8-13` awaits `fetch('/api/subscribe', …)` but never looks at the response. `fetch` only rejects on network failure, so a `422` falls straight through to the success message. The `catch` branch only handles network errors.
+
+The server (`server.js:8-12`) rejects addresses that the browser's `<input type="email">` check accepts, so real users get there:
+
+| Address | Browser validation | API response | Page shows |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| `reader@example.com` | passes | `201 {"ok":true}` | "You're subscribed…" ✅ |
+| `jane+news@example.com` | passes | `422 {"error":"Addresses with + are not supported."}` | "You're subscribed…" ❌ |
+| `jane@localhost` (no TLD) | passes | `422 {"error":"Enter a valid email address."}` | "You're subscribed…" ❌ |
+
+Plus-addressing (`name+tag@gmail.com`) is common, so it's the likely source of the reports.
+
+## How it was checked
+
+- Drove the real page in headless Chromium (Playwright): opened `/`, filled the **Email** field, clicked **Subscribe**, then recorded the `/api/subscribe` response and the `role=status` text.
+- Console: on each rejected submit the only error was the browser's `Failed to load resource: the server responded with a status of 422 (Unprocessable Entity)`. The page logs nothing else and shows no error. No requests failed at the network level.
+
+## Regression test
+
+`e2e/subscribe.test.js` (node:test plus Playwright; starts `node server.js` on a free port and stops it afterwards).
+
+Run: `node --test e2e/subscribe.test.js`
+
+- **Control:** a valid address returns 201 and the page says subscribed. This passes.
+- **Bug:** for `jane+news@example.com` and `jane@localhost`, it checks that the API returned 422 and then asserts the page does **not** say "subscribed" and does show the server's error message.
+
+Current result against the app as it is: **1 pass, 2 fail**, with failures like:
+
+```
+server rejected with "Addresses with + are not supported." but the page told the user: "You're subscribed. Check your inbox for the first issue."
+```
+
+To check that the test can pass, I ran it against a scratch copy of the app with `app.js` changed to check `res.ok` and show `error` from the response body. All 3 tests passed. The app in this repo was **not** changed.
+
+## Suggested fix (not applied)
+
+In `public/app.js`, keep the response, and when `!res.ok`, show the server's `error` text instead of the success message. Separately, decide whether rejecting `+` addresses is intended. If it isn't, the server rule in `server.js:10` is a product bug too.
+
+## Other observations (not verified as causes)
+
+- There's no email-sending code in this repo. Subscribers go into an in-memory `Set` that is lost when the server restarts. If accepted users also report getting no email, the cause is outside this codebase or in this persistence gap.
+- Not run: the skill's mutation check (`mutate.mjs`). It mutates app lines changed against `HEAD`, and this task changes no app code.
